{"pages": [{"input_path": "/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-martyall-code-cryptoindex/1fa166ed-7045-4dc7-976a-476cbb70b7c2/scratchpad/smoke-erickson.pdf", "page_index": 0, "page_count": 3, "width": 964, "height": 1384, "model_settings": {"use_doc_preprocessor": false, "use_layout_detection": true, "use_chart_recognition": false, "use_seal_recognition": false, "use_ocr_for_image_block": false, "format_block_content": false, "merge_layout_blocks": true, "markdown_ignore_labels": ["number", "footnote", "header", "header_image", "footer", "footer_image", "aside_text"], "return_layout_polygon_points": true}, "parsing_res_list": [{"block_label": "header_image", "block_content": "", "block_bbox": [563, 61, 586, 85], "block_id": 0, "block_order": null, "group_id": 0, "block_polygon_points": [[563.0, 61.0], [585.0, 61.0], [585.0, 84.0], [563.0, 84.0]]}, {"block_label": "header", "block_content": "3.2. Aside: Even Faster Fibonacci Numbers", "block_bbox": [563, 64, 920, 87], "block_id": 1, "block_order": null, "group_id": 1, "block_polygon_points": [[573.0, 64.0], [573.0, 81.0], [583.0, 86.0], [919.0, 86.0], [919.0, 64.0]]}, {"block_label": "algorithm", "block_content": "ITERFIBO2(n):\nprev \u2190 1\ncurr \u2190 0\nfor i \u2190 1 to n\n    next \u2190 curr + prev\n    prev \u2190 curr\n    curr \u2190 next\nreturn curr", "block_bbox": [382, 145, 617, 337], "block_id": 2, "block_order": 1, "group_id": 2, "block_polygon_points": [[382.0, 145.0], [617.0, 145.0], [617.0, 337.0], [382.0, 337.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "(This algorithm uses the non-standard but consistent base case  $ F_{-1} = 1 $ so that ITERFIBO2(0) returns the correct value 0.) Although saving space can be absolutely crucial in practice, we won't focus on space issues in this book.", "block_bbox": [138, 354, 859, 437], "block_id": 3, "block_order": 2, "group_id": 3, "block_polygon_points": [[138.0, 354.0], [859.0, 354.0], [859.0, 437.0], [138.0, 437.0]]}, {"block_label": "paragraph_title", "block_content": "3.2 Aside:3.2 Even Faster Fibonacci Numbers", "block_bbox": [127, 473, 657, 503], "block_id": 4, "block_order": 3, "group_id": 4, "block_polygon_points": [[127.0, 473.0], [657.0, 473.0], [657.0, 503.0], [127.0, 503.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "Although the previous algorithm is simple and attractive, it is not the fastest algorithm to compute Fibonacci numbers. We can derive a faster algorithm by exploiting the following matrix reformulation of the Fibonacci recurrence:", "block_bbox": [137, 526, 861, 608], "block_id": 5, "block_order": 4, "group_id": 5, "block_polygon_points": [[137.0, 526.0], [861.0, 526.0], [861.0, 608.0], [137.0, 608.0]]}, {"block_label": "display_formula", "block_content": " $$ \\begin{bmatrix}0&1\\\\ 1&1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x\\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\\\ x+y\\end{bmatrix} $$ ", "block_bbox": [393, 621, 606, 685], "block_id": 6, "block_order": 5, "group_id": 6, "block_polygon_points": [[393.0, 621.0], [606.0, 621.0], [606.0, 685.0], [393.0, 685.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "In other words, multiplying a two-dimensional vector by the matrix  $ [0\\ 1] $ has exactly the same effect as one iteration of the inner loop of ITERFIBO2. It follows that multiplying by the matrix  $ n $ times is the same as iterating the loop  $ n $ times:", "block_bbox": [139, 704, 861, 787], "block_id": 7, "block_order": 6, "group_id": 7, "block_polygon_points": [[139.0, 704.0], [861.0, 704.0], [861.0, 787.0], [139.0, 787.0]]}, {"block_label": "display_formula", "block_content": " $$ \\begin{bmatrix}0&1\\\\ 1&1\\end{bmatrix}^{n}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}F_{n-1}\\\\ F_{n}\\end{bmatrix}. $$ ", "block_bbox": [389, 800, 607, 866], "block_id": 8, "block_order": 7, "group_id": 8, "block_polygon_points": [[389.0, 800.0], [607.0, 800.0], [607.0, 866.0], [389.0, 866.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "So if we want the $n$th Fibonacci number, we only need to compute the $n$th power of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix}$. If we use repeated squaring,$^9$ computing the $n$th power of something requires only $O(\\log n)$ multiplications. Here, because \u201csomething\u201d is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, that means $O(\\log n)$ $2 \\times 2$ matrix multiplications, each of which reduces to a constant number of integer multiplications and additions. Thus, we can compute $F_n$ in only $O(\\log n)$ integer arithmetic operations.", "block_bbox": [136, 882, 861, 1043], "block_id": 9, "block_order": 8, "group_id": 9, "block_polygon_points": [[136.0, 882.0], [861.0, 882.0], [861.0, 1043.0], [136.0, 1043.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "We can achieve the same speedup using the identity  $ F_n = F_m F_{n-m-1} + F_{m+1} F_{n-m} $, which holds (by induction!) for all integers  $ m $ and  $ n $. In particular, this identity implies the following mutual recurrence for pairs of adjacent Fibonacci numbers, first proposed by \u00c9douard Lucas in 1898:", "block_bbox": [137, 1046, 862, 1152], "block_id": 10, "block_order": 9, "group_id": 10, "block_polygon_points": [[137.0, 1046.0], [862.0, 1046.0], [862.0, 1152.0], [137.0, 1152.0]]}, {"block_label": "display_formula", "block_content": " $ F_{2n-1}=F_{n-1}^{2}+F_{n}^{2} $", "block_bbox": [304, 1171, 478, 1204], "block_id": 11, "block_order": 10, "group_id": 11, "block_polygon_points": [[304.0, 1171.0], [478.0, 1171.0], [478.0, 1204.0], [304.0, 1204.0]]}, {"block_label": "display_formula", "block_content": "F_{2n}=F_{n}(F_{n-1}+F_{n+1})=F_{n}(2F_{n-1}+F_{n})\\]", "block_bbox": [323, 1212, 695, 1239], "block_id": 12, "block_order": 11, "group_id": 12, "block_polygon_points": [[323.0, 1212.0], [695.0, 1212.0], [695.0, 1239.0], [323.0, 1239.0]]}, {"block_label": "footnote", "block_content": "\u2079as suggested by Pi\u00f1gala for powers of 2 elsewhere in Chanda\u1e25\u015bastra", "block_bbox": [170, 1260, 695, 1284], "block_id": 13, "block_order": null, "group_id": 13, "block_polygon_points": [[170.0, 1260.0], [695.0, 1260.0], [695.0, 1284.0], [170.0, 1284.0]]}, {"block_label": "number", "block_content": "103", "block_bbox": [888, 1317, 921, 1336], "block_id": 14, "block_order": null, "group_id": 14, "block_polygon_points": [[888.0, 1317.0], [920.0, 1317.0], [920.0, 1335.0], [888.0, 1335.0]]}], "layout_det_res": {"input_path": null, "page_index": null, "boxes": [{"cls_id": 13, "label": "header_image", "score": 0.38866376876831055, "coordinate": [563, 61, 586, 85], "order": null, "polygon_points": [[563.0, 61.0], [585.0, 61.0], [585.0, 84.0], [563.0, 84.0]]}, {"cls_id": 12, "label": "header", "score": 0.8768388628959656, "coordinate": [563, 64, 920, 87], "order": null, "polygon_points": [[573.0, 64.0], [573.0, 81.0], [583.0, 86.0], [919.0, 86.0], [919.0, 64.0]]}, {"cls_id": 1, "label": "algorithm", "score": 0.923829972743988, "coordinate": [382, 145, 617, 337], "order": 1, "polygon_points": [[382.0, 145.0], [617.0, 145.0], [617.0, 337.0], [382.0, 337.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.937228262424469, "coordinate": [138, 354, 859, 437], "order": 2, "polygon_points": [[138.0, 354.0], [859.0, 354.0], [859.0, 437.0], [138.0, 437.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.8165985345840454, "coordinate": [750, 356, 827, 378], "order": 3, "polygon_points": [[750.0, 356.0], [750.0, 377.0], [826.0, 377.0], [826.0, 362.0], [822.0, 356.0]]}, {"cls_id": 17, "label": "paragraph_title", "score": 0.8730063438415527, "coordinate": [127, 473, 657, 503], "order": 4, "polygon_points": [[127.0, 473.0], [657.0, 473.0], [657.0, 503.0], [127.0, 503.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.9467041492462158, "coordinate": [137, 526, 861, 608], "order": 5, "polygon_points": [[137.0, 526.0], [861.0, 526.0], [861.0, 608.0], [137.0, 608.0]]}, {"cls_id": 5, "label": "display_formula", "score": 0.942656397819519, "coordinate": [393, 621, 606, 685], "order": 6, "polygon_points": [[393.0, 621.0], [606.0, 621.0], [606.0, 685.0], [393.0, 685.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.9425289034843445, "coordinate": [139, 704, 861, 787], "order": 7, "polygon_points": [[139.0, 704.0], [861.0, 704.0], [861.0, 787.0], [139.0, 787.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.7467588782310486, "coordinate": [775, 702, 819, 732], "order": 8, "polygon_points": [[775.0, 702.0], [775.0, 731.0], [818.0, 731.0], [818.0, 702.0]]}, {"cls_id": 5, "label": "display_formula", "score": 0.9372251033782959, "coordinate": [389, 800, 607, 866], "order": 9, "polygon_points": [[389.0, 800.0], [607.0, 800.0], [607.0, 866.0], [389.0, 866.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.9607537388801575, "coordinate": [136, 882, 861, 1043], "order": 10, "polygon_points": [[136.0, 882.0], [861.0, 882.0], [861.0, 1043.0], [136.0, 1043.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.6954408884048462, "coordinate": [267, 905, 311, 936], "order": 11, "polygon_points": [[267.0, 905.0], [267.0, 935.0], [310.0, 935.0], [310.0, 905.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.4979061782360077, "coordinate": [157, 964, 207, 984], "order": 12, "polygon_points": [[157.0, 964.0], [206.0, 964.0], [206.0, 983.0], [157.0, 983.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.5834602117538452, "coordinate": [388, 963, 517, 986], "order": 13, "polygon_points": [[388.0, 963.0], [516.0, 963.0], [516.0, 985.0], [388.0, 985.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.7686742544174194, "coordinate": [295, 1019, 318, 1041], "order": 14, "polygon_points": [[295.0, 1019.0], [317.0, 1019.0], [317.0, 1040.0], [295.0, 1040.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.9593879580497742, "coordinate": [137, 1046, 862, 1152], "order": 15, "polygon_points": [[137.0, 1046.0], [862.0, 1046.0], [862.0, 1152.0], [137.0, 1152.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.8762614130973816, "coordinate": [691, 1047, 859, 1071], "order": 16, "polygon_points": [[691.0, 1047.0], [859.0, 1047.0], [859.0, 1071.0], [691.0, 1071.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.8663268089294434, "coordinate": [142, 1074, 235, 1099], "order": 17, "polygon_points": [[142.0, 1074.0], [234.0, 1074.0], [234.0, 1098.0], [142.0, 1098.0]]}, {"cls_id": 5, "label": "display_formula", "score": 0.8660898804664612, "coordinate": [304, 1171, 478, 1204], "order": 18, "polygon_points": [[304.0, 1171.0], [478.0, 1171.0], [478.0, 1204.0], [304.0, 1204.0]]}, {"cls_id": 5, "label": "display_formula", "score": 0.857277512550354, "coordinate": [323, 1212, 695, 1239], "order": 19, "polygon_points": [[323.0, 1212.0], [695.0, 1212.0], [695.0, 1239.0], [323.0, 1239.0]]}, {"cls_id": 10, "label": "footnote", "score": 0.8917779922485352, "coordinate": [170, 1260, 695, 1284], "order": null, "polygon_points": [[170.0, 1260.0], [695.0, 1260.0], [695.0, 1284.0], [170.0, 1284.0]]}, {"cls_id": 16, "label": "number", "score": 0.793107807636261, "coordinate": [888, 1317, 921, 1336], "order": 20, "polygon_points": [[888.0, 1317.0], [920.0, 1317.0], [920.0, 1335.0], [888.0, 1335.0]]}]}}, {"input_path": "/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-martyall-code-cryptoindex/1fa166ed-7045-4dc7-976a-476cbb70b7c2/scratchpad/smoke-erickson.pdf", "page_index": 1, "page_count": 3, "width": 964, "height": 1384, "model_settings": {"use_doc_preprocessor": false, "use_layout_detection": true, "use_chart_recognition": false, "use_seal_recognition": false, "use_ocr_for_image_block": false, "format_block_content": false, "merge_layout_blocks": true, "markdown_ignore_labels": ["number", "footnote", "header", "header_image", "footer", "footer_image", "aside_text"], "return_layout_polygon_points": true}, "parsing_res_list": [{"block_label": "header", "block_content": "3. DYNAMIC PROGRAMMING", "block_bbox": [42, 67, 259, 87], "block_id": 0, "block_order": null, "group_id": 0, "block_polygon_points": [[42.0, 67.0], [258.0, 67.0], [258.0, 86.0], [42.0, 86.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "i) These recurrences translate directly into the following algorithm: (We can also derive this mutual recurrence directly from the matrix-squaring algorithm.)", "block_bbox": [102, 147, 824, 202], "block_id": 1, "block_order": 1, "group_id": 1, "block_polygon_points": [[102.0, 147.0], [824.0, 147.0], [824.0, 202.0], [102.0, 202.0]]}, {"block_label": "algorithm", "block_content": "\u300aCompute the pair $F_{n-1}, F_n$\u300b\n$\\frac{\\text{FASTRecFIBO}(n)}{\\text{if } n=1}$\nreturn 0,1\n$m \\leftarrow \\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$\n$hprv, hcur \\leftarrow \\text{FASTRecFIBO}(m)$ $\\langle F_{m-1}, F_m \\rangle$\n$prev \\leftarrow hprv^2 + hcur^2$ $\\langle F_{2m-1} \\rangle$\n$curr \\leftarrow hcur \\cdot (2 \\cdot hprv + hcur)$ $\\langle F_{2m} \\rangle$\n$next \\leftarrow prev + curr$ $\\langle F_{2m+1} \\rangle$\nif $n$ is even\n    return prev,curr\nelse\n    return curr,next", "block_bbox": [259, 223, 667, 550], "block_id": 2, "block_order": 2, "group_id": 2, "block_polygon_points": [[259.0, 223.0], [667.0, 223.0], [667.0, 550.0], [259.0, 550.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "i\nOur standard recursion tree technique implies that this algorithm performs only  $ O(\\log n) $ integer arithmetic operations.", "block_bbox": [102, 574, 824, 628], "block_id": 3, "block_order": 3, "group_id": 3, "block_polygon_points": [[102.0, 574.0], [824.0, 574.0], [824.0, 628.0], [102.0, 628.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "This is an exponential speedup over the standard iterative algorithm, which was already an exponential speedup over our original recursive algorithm. Right?", "block_bbox": [102, 631, 824, 712], "block_id": 4, "block_order": 4, "group_id": 4, "block_polygon_points": [[102.0, 631.0], [824.0, 631.0], [824.0, 712.0], [102.0, 712.0]]}, {"block_label": "paragraph_title", "block_content": "Whoa! Not so fast!", "block_bbox": [103, 747, 290, 772], "block_id": 5, "block_order": 5, "group_id": 5, "block_polygon_points": [[103.0, 747.0], [290.0, 747.0], [290.0, 772.0], [103.0, 772.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "Well, not exactly. Fibonacci numbers grow exponentially fast. The  $ n $th Fibonacci number is approximately  $ n \\log_{10} \\phi \\approx n/5 $ decimal digits long, or  $ n \\log_2 \\phi \\approx 2n/3 $ bits. So we can't possibly compute  $ F_n $ in logarithmic time \u2014 we need  $ \\Omega(n) $ time just to write down the answer!", "block_bbox": [102, 790, 823, 898], "block_id": 6, "block_order": 6, "group_id": 6, "block_polygon_points": [[102.0, 790.0], [823.0, 790.0], [823.0, 898.0], [102.0, 898.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "The way out of this apparent paradox is to observe that we can't perform arbitrary-precision arithmetic in constant time. Let  $ M(n) $ denote the time required to multiply two n-digit numbers. The running time of FASTRECFIBO satisfies the recurrence  $ T(n) = T(\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor) + M(n) $, which solves to  $ T(n) = O(M(n)) $ via recursion trees. The fastest integer multiplication algorithm known (as of 2019) runs in  $ O(n \\log n) $ time, so that is also the running time of the fastest algorithm known (as of 2019) to compute Fibonacci numbers.", "block_bbox": [102, 902, 824, 1089], "block_id": 7, "block_order": 7, "group_id": 7, "block_polygon_points": [[102.0, 902.0], [824.0, 902.0], [824.0, 1089.0], [102.0, 1089.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "Is this algorithm slower than our \u201clinear-time\u201d iterative algorithms? Actually, no\u2014addition isn\u2019t free, either! Adding two n-digit numbers requires  $ O(n) $ time, so the iterative algorithms ITERFIBO and ITERFIBO2 actually run in  $ O(n^2) $ time. (Do you see why?) So FASTRECFIBO is significantly faster than the iterative algorithms, just not exponentially faster.", "block_bbox": [103, 1093, 825, 1228], "block_id": 8, "block_order": 8, "group_id": 8, "block_polygon_points": [[103.0, 1093.0], [825.0, 1093.0], [825.0, 1228.0], [103.0, 1228.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "In the original recursive algorithm, the extra cost of arbitrary-precision arithmetic is overwhelmed by the huge number of recursive calls. The correct", "block_bbox": [102, 1231, 825, 1284], "block_id": 9, "block_order": 9, "group_id": 9, "block_polygon_points": [[102.0, 1231.0], [825.0, 1231.0], [825.0, 1284.0], [102.0, 1284.0]]}, {"block_label": "number", "block_content": "104", "block_bbox": [43, 1318, 78, 1335], "block_id": 10, "block_order": null, "group_id": 10, "block_polygon_points": [[43.0, 1318.0], [77.0, 1318.0], [77.0, 1334.0], [43.0, 1334.0]]}], "layout_det_res": {"input_path": null, "page_index": null, "boxes": [{"cls_id": 12, "label": "header", "score": 0.876822292804718, "coordinate": [42, 67, 259, 87], "order": null, "polygon_points": [[42.0, 67.0], [258.0, 67.0], [258.0, 86.0], [42.0, 86.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.9318892359733582, "coordinate": [102, 147, 824, 202], "order": 1, "polygon_points": [[102.0, 147.0], [824.0, 147.0], [824.0, 202.0], [102.0, 202.0]]}, {"cls_id": 1, "label": "algorithm", "score": 0.9781555533409119, "coordinate": [259, 223, 667, 550], "order": 2, "polygon_points": [[259.0, 223.0], [667.0, 223.0], [667.0, 550.0], [259.0, 550.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.9340458512306213, "coordinate": [102, 574, 824, 628], "order": 3, "polygon_points": [[102.0, 574.0], [824.0, 574.0], [824.0, 628.0], [102.0, 628.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.30677786469459534, "coordinate": [106, 601, 183, 626], "order": 4, "polygon_points": [[106.0, 601.0], [106.0, 625.0], [182.0, 625.0], [182.0, 601.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.936735212802887, "coordinate": [102, 631, 824, 712], "order": 5, "polygon_points": [[102.0, 631.0], [824.0, 631.0], [824.0, 712.0], [102.0, 712.0]]}, {"cls_id": 17, "label": "paragraph_title", "score": 0.8529932498931885, "coordinate": [103, 747, 290, 772], "order": 6, "polygon_points": [[103.0, 747.0], [290.0, 747.0], [290.0, 772.0], [103.0, 772.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.9517377614974976, "coordinate": [102, 790, 823, 898], "order": 7, "polygon_points": [[102.0, 790.0], [823.0, 790.0], [823.0, 898.0], [102.0, 898.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.7625699639320374, "coordinate": [344, 818, 472, 844], "order": 8, "polygon_points": [[344.0, 818.0], [472.0, 818.0], [472.0, 844.0], [344.0, 844.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.7615786790847778, "coordinate": [675, 817, 820, 843], "order": 9, "polygon_points": [[675.0, 817.0], [820.0, 817.0], [820.0, 843.0], [675.0, 843.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.7383242845535278, "coordinate": [419, 848, 442, 870], "order": 10, "polygon_points": [[419.0, 848.0], [441.0, 848.0], [441.0, 869.0], [419.0, 869.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.968193769454956, "coordinate": [102, 902, 824, 1089], "order": 11, "polygon_points": [[102.0, 902.0], [824.0, 902.0], [824.0, 1089.0], [102.0, 1089.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.7890163660049438, "coordinate": [336, 981, 575, 1007], "order": 12, "polygon_points": [[336.0, 981.0], [575.0, 981.0], [575.0, 1007.0], [336.0, 1007.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.4793338477611542, "coordinate": [748, 982, 820, 1006], "order": 13, "polygon_points": [[748.0, 982.0], [819.0, 982.0], [819.0, 1005.0], [748.0, 1005.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.9580205678939819, "coordinate": [103, 1093, 825, 1228], "order": 14, "polygon_points": [[103.0, 1093.0], [825.0, 1093.0], [825.0, 1228.0], [103.0, 1228.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.5690436363220215, "coordinate": [710, 1147, 768, 1171], "order": 15, "polygon_points": [[710.0, 1147.0], [710.0, 1170.0], [767.0, 1170.0], [767.0, 1147.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.9290522933006287, "coordinate": [102, 1231, 825, 1284], "order": 16, "polygon_points": [[102.0, 1231.0], [825.0, 1231.0], [825.0, 1284.0], [102.0, 1284.0]]}, {"cls_id": 16, "label": "number", "score": 0.7792068719863892, "coordinate": [43, 1318, 78, 1335], "order": 17, "polygon_points": [[43.0, 1318.0], [77.0, 1318.0], [77.0, 1334.0], [43.0, 1334.0]]}]}}, {"input_path": "/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-martyall-code-cryptoindex/1fa166ed-7045-4dc7-976a-476cbb70b7c2/scratchpad/smoke-erickson.pdf", "page_index": 2, "page_count": 3, "width": 964, "height": 1384, "model_settings": {"use_doc_preprocessor": false, "use_layout_detection": true, "use_chart_recognition": false, "use_seal_recognition": false, "use_ocr_for_image_block": false, "format_block_content": false, "merge_layout_blocks": true, "markdown_ignore_labels": ["number", "footnote", "header", "header_image", "footer", "footer_image", "aside_text"], "return_layout_polygon_points": true}, "parsing_res_list": [{"block_label": "header", "block_content": "3.3. Interpunctio Verborum Redux", "block_bbox": [652, 66, 921, 88], "block_id": 0, "block_order": null, "group_id": 0, "block_polygon_points": [[652.0, 66.0], [921.0, 66.0], [921.0, 88.0], [652.0, 88.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "i t i r e c u r r e n c e i s T(n)=T(n-1)+T(n-2)+O(n), w h i c h s t i l l h a s t h e s o l u t i o n T(n)=O(\\phi^{n}).", "block_bbox": [139, 147, 859, 200], "block_id": 1, "block_order": 1, "group_id": 1, "block_polygon_points": [[139.0, 147.0], [859.0, 147.0], [859.0, 200.0], [139.0, 200.0]]}, {"block_label": "paragraph_title", "block_content": "3.3 Interpunctio Verborum Redux", "block_bbox": [138, 244, 544, 275], "block_id": 2, "block_order": 2, "group_id": 2, "block_polygon_points": [[138.0, 244.0], [544.0, 244.0], [544.0, 275.0], [138.0, 275.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "For our next dynamic programming algorithm, let's consider the text segmentation problem from the previous chapter. We are given a string  $ A[1..n] $ and a subroutine IsWORD that determines whether a given string is a word (whatever that means), and we want to know whether A can be partitioned into a sequence of words.", "block_bbox": [138, 299, 861, 433], "block_id": 3, "block_order": 3, "group_id": 3, "block_polygon_points": [[138.0, 299.0], [861.0, 299.0], [861.0, 433.0], [138.0, 433.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "We solved this problem by defining a function Splittable(i) that returns TRUE if and only if the suffix A[i..n] can be partitioned into a sequence of words. We need to compute Splittable(1). This function satisfies the recurrence", "block_bbox": [139, 437, 859, 518], "block_id": 4, "block_order": 4, "group_id": 4, "block_polygon_points": [[139.0, 437.0], [859.0, 437.0], [859.0, 518.0], [139.0, 518.0]]}, {"block_label": "display_formula", "block_content": "Splittable(i)=\\begin{cases}TruE&if i>n\\\\\\bigvee_{j=i}^{n}\\left(IsWord(i,j)\\land Splittable(j+1)\\right)&otherwise\\end{cases}\\]", "block_bbox": [197, 543, 797, 644], "block_id": 5, "block_order": 5, "group_id": 5, "block_polygon_points": [[197.0, 543.0], [797.0, 543.0], [797.0, 644.0], [197.0, 644.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "where  $  \\text{IsWord}(i, j)  $ is shorthand for  $  \\text{IsWord}(A[i \\ldots j])  $. This recurrence translates directly into a recursive backtracking algorithm that calls the  $  \\text{IsWord}  $ subroutine  $  O(2^n)  $ times in the worst case.", "block_bbox": [136, 667, 859, 747], "block_id": 6, "block_order": 6, "group_id": 6, "block_polygon_points": [[136.0, 667.0], [859.0, 667.0], [859.0, 747.0], [136.0, 747.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "But for any fixed string  $ A[1..n] $, there are only  $ n $ different ways to call the recursive function  $ Splittable(i) $\u2014one for each value of  $ i $ between 1 and  $ n+1 $\u2014and only  $ O(n^2) $ different ways to call  $ IsWORD(i,j) $\u2014one for each pair  $ (i,j) $ such that  $ 1 \\leq i \\leq j \\leq n $. Why are we spending exponential time computing only a polynomial amount of stuff?", "block_bbox": [137, 751, 860, 887], "block_id": 7, "block_order": 7, "group_id": 7, "block_polygon_points": [[137.0, 751.0], [860.0, 751.0], [860.0, 887.0], [137.0, 887.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "Each recursive subproblem is specified by an integer between 1 and  $ n+1 $, so we can memoize the function Splittable into an array SplitTable[1..n+1]. Each subproblem Splittable(i) depends only on results of subproblems Splittable(j) where j>i, so the memoized recursive algorithm fills the array in decreasing index order. If we fill the array in this order deliberately, we obtain the dynamic programming algorithm shown in Figure 3.3. The algorithm makes  $ O(n^2) $ calls to IsWORD, an exponential improvement over our earlier backtracking algorithm.", "block_bbox": [137, 890, 860, 1106], "block_id": 8, "block_order": 8, "group_id": 8, "block_polygon_points": [[137.0, 890.0], [860.0, 890.0], [860.0, 1106.0], [137.0, 1106.0]]}, {"block_label": "paragraph_title", "block_content": "i3.4 The Pattern: Smart Recursion", "block_bbox": [138, 1150, 555, 1180], "block_id": 9, "block_order": 9, "group_id": 9, "block_polygon_points": [[138.0, 1150.0], [555.0, 1150.0], [555.0, 1180.0], [138.0, 1180.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "In a nutshell, dynamic programming is recursion without repetition. Dynamic programming algorithms store the solutions of intermediate subproblems, often but not always in some kind of array or table. Many algorithms students", "block_bbox": [137, 1204, 861, 1284], "block_id": 10, "block_order": 10, "group_id": 10, "block_polygon_points": [[137.0, 1204.0], [861.0, 1204.0], [861.0, 1284.0], [137.0, 1284.0]]}, {"block_label": "number", "block_content": "105", "block_bbox": [887, 1317, 921, 1336], "block_id": 11, "block_order": null, "group_id": 11, "block_polygon_points": [[887.0, 1317.0], [920.0, 1317.0], [920.0, 1335.0], [887.0, 1335.0]]}], "layout_det_res": {"input_path": null, "page_index": null, "boxes": [{"cls_id": 12, "label": "header", "score": 0.8863295316696167, "coordinate": [652, 66, 921, 88], "order": null, "polygon_points": [[652.0, 66.0], [921.0, 66.0], [921.0, 88.0], [652.0, 88.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.9101173281669617, "coordinate": [139, 147, 859, 200], "order": 1, "polygon_points": [[139.0, 147.0], [859.0, 147.0], [859.0, 200.0], [139.0, 200.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.7588843107223511, "coordinate": [268, 148, 592, 171], "order": 2, "polygon_points": [[268.0, 148.0], [592.0, 148.0], [592.0, 171.0], [268.0, 171.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.80610591173172, "coordinate": [142, 173, 272, 199], "order": 3, "polygon_points": [[142.0, 173.0], [272.0, 173.0], [272.0, 199.0], [142.0, 199.0]]}, {"cls_id": 17, "label": "paragraph_title", "score": 0.8855221271514893, "coordinate": [138, 244, 544, 275], "order": 4, "polygon_points": [[138.0, 244.0], [544.0, 244.0], [544.0, 275.0], [138.0, 275.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.9525458216667175, "coordinate": [138, 299, 861, 433], "order": 5, "polygon_points": [[138.0, 299.0], [861.0, 299.0], [861.0, 433.0], [138.0, 433.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.4597682058811188, "coordinate": [725, 326, 798, 351], "order": 6, "polygon_points": [[725.0, 326.0], [725.0, 350.0], [797.0, 350.0], [797.0, 345.0], [797.0, 338.0], [797.0, 326.0]]}, {"cls_id": 22, "label": "text", "score": 0.9388797283172607, "coordinate": [139, 437, 859, 518], "order": 7, "polygon_points": [[139.0, 437.0], [859.0, 437.0], [859.0, 518.0], [139.0, 518.0]]}, {"cls_id": 15, "label": "inline_formula", "score": 0.4048677086830139, "coordinate": [350, 464, 419, 489], "order": 8, "polygon_points": [[350.0, 464.0], [350.0, 488.0], [418.0, 488.0], [418.0, 464.0]]}, {"cls_id": 5, "label": "display_formula", "score": 0.942279040813446, "coordinate": [197, 543, 797, 644], "order": 9, "polygon_points": [[197.0, 543.0], [797.0, 543.0], [79